      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Interleukin-25 has been implicated in the pathogenesis of asthma from studies on human asthmatics and in murine asthma models."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "In this study, we hypothesized that chronic exposure of the airways to IL-25 alone is able to induce pathogenic changes observed in animal models of asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We performed a detailed comparison of the dynamics of development of cellular infiltration, cytokine expression and airways remodelling and hyperresponsiveness in mice sensitized and challenged with OVA, a classical model of allergic asthma and those exposed to IL-25 alone."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Intranasal challenge of BALB/c mice with IL-25 alone induced a delayed (compared with OVA-challenge), predominantly eosinophilic and lymphocytic infiltration into the airways lumen, along with increased production of Th2-type cytokines, chemokines and collagen, secretion of epithelial mucus, goblet cell hyperplasia, deposition of subepithelial collagen, airways smooth muscle cell hyperplasia and angiogenesis. Correspondingly, IL-25 as well as OVA challenge both induced airways hyperresponsiveness and increased lung tissue damping. In contrast, IL-25 exposure did not increase IgE or IgG1 production."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS AND CLINICAL RELEVANCE",
            "content": "The data suggest that chronic airways exposure to IL-25 alone is sufficient to induce allergen- and IgE-independent, asthma-like airways inflammation, remodelling and hyperresponsiveness in mice. Thus, IL-25 is a key molecular target in asthma, irrespective of the coexistence of IgE-dependent mechanisms."
          }
        ]
      },
